👮 **Wachiman** · review of the whole pull request, at `f767aef`: all 7 files reviewed.

5 finding(s): 3 to fix, 2 to consider; 4 on their lines. Reply in a comment's thread to answer it.

### Outside the diff

- 🟠🐛 **Fix · bug: Cancelled file is still uploaded: the abort signal no longer reaches the attachments POST or the restore wait (confidence high)** (`frontend/src/renderer/hooks/useConversation.ts:2433`): The diff removes `signal,` from the `/attachments` POST, and the new `await Promise.all(restores.map((restore) => restore.continue()))` at line 2428 ignores the signal as well. `signal` is still passed to `toWireAttachments`, but that only aborts the large-file upload (`attachment-uploads.ts:72-74`). Inline-data files such as pasted images don't use it. So a user who cancels a pending attachment while the restore runs (the 'Attaching x' item, `useFileAttachments.ts:545-555`) still gets the file POSTed into the restored worktree once the restore ends. Their cancelled file is written there and can't be aborted. The PR body says 'A file removed while it waits is never uploaded', and the code doesn't do that. No test in the diff covers it. I found no 'removed' or abort test in `useConversation.test.tsx`; the only signal assertions are at lines 1237 and 2006. Fix: check `signal?.aborted` after the wait and before the POST (throw an AbortError), and pass `signal` to the POST again. Add a test where a file is cancelled during the restore and the attachments POST is never made.

### Summary

I found two bugs and one regression in how Enter is held, plus a lower-confidence question about `continue()`. All come from replacing the old restore-tracking and held-file machinery with simpler gates. The abort signal no longer reaches the attachments POST or the restore wait, so a file the user cancels during the restore is still uploaded. That contradicts the PR body's 'a file removed while it waits is never uploaded', and no test covers it. Enter on a file-only draft is held only while a file is still being read, so a file that has finished staging gets no hold and the Enter does nothing. A held file-only Enter no longer checks that the files are unchanged, so it can send a different file the user attached afterwards. I also noted that `Mutation.continue()` may not be a safe way to wait for a restore, and that several tests for the removed behaviour were dropped with no replacement. I read the code only; I did not run it.

